        {
          "id": 1928876,
          "username": "Muhammad_Saleem_Baba",
          "avatar_template": "https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/m/97f17d/{size}.png",
          "created_at": "2026-11-02T06:56:17.853Z",
          "cooked": "<p>What’s happening here is mechanical, and it’s worth understanding before trusting any checker output. When Shopify imports a CSV, the rows for a handle are treated as the complete definition of that product. If a variant row is missing, or a row carries SKU and weight but no Option1 Name and Option1 Value, Shopify doesn’t warn you — it rebuilds the product from the rows it can read, and the variants that don’t parse simply stop existing. Images go the same way: rows that aren’t attached cleanly get dropped without an error.</p>\n<p>A preflight that protects you:</p>\n<p>1. Export the live catalog first and save it untouched as your rollback.</p>\n<p>2. For every handle, count variant rows in your import file against the live export. Any mismatch is a stop sign.</p>\n<p>3. Check that every variant row has Handle, Option1 Name and Option1 Value filled — a SKU alone doesn’t keep a variant alive.</p>\n<p>4. Test the import on one product first, then compare variant and image counts after.</p>\n<p>That row-count comparison is the check I run on every repair file before it goes back — most silent deletions are visible in the counts before Shopify sees the file.</p>",
